      "id": "ARCH-009",
      "decision": "Add a catch-all 404 route and NotFoundView.vue with a role-aware 'Back to home' link.",
      "rationale": "Today the router has no catch-all; an unknown path lands on a blank `<router-view />` inside App.vue with the nav still rendered — confusing because nothing tells the user the path is bad. A typed 404 route lets the brutalist UI explicitly tell users where they are, and the role-aware home link plays well with ARCH-007's auth store. Keeping it as a separate view (not an inline template in App.vue) keeps App.vue dumb and aligns with the 'App renders router-view, nothing else' rule from ARCH-007.",
      "affects": [
        "frontend/src/components/NotFoundView.vue",
        "frontend/src/router.ts"
      ],
      "design_notes": "CREATE `frontend/src/components/NotFoundView.vue`:\n  - Brutalist card: thick 2px black border, white background, monospace mark `404` styled like the `.logo-mark` (border + mono).\n  - Heading: 'Page not found'.\n  - Body: 'The path \"{{ route.fullPath }}\" does not exist.' (use `useRoute()` to show the attempted path verbatim — helps debugging).\n  - Primary link: text 'Back to home' that resolves the target via `useAuth()`:\n    * if not logged in → `/login`\n    * STUDENT → `/student`\n    * LECTURER or ADMIN → `/lecturer`\n    Use `<router-link>` not raw `<a>`.\n  - Optional secondary action: 'Go back' that calls `router.back()` (only if `window.history.length > 1`).\n  - Reuse the `.card`, `.btn-primary` styles already established; do not invent new classes beyond a small `.notfound-shell` wrapper centering the card vertically/horizontally similar to `.login-shell`.\n\nROUTE REGISTRATION (`router.ts`):\n  - Append AT THE END of the routes array:\n    `{ path: '/:pathMatch(.*)*', name: 'not-found', component: () => import('./components/NotFoundView.vue'), meta: { requiresAuth: false } }`\n  - `meta.requiresAuth: false` is important: an unauthenticated user typing a junk URL should see 404, not be punted to /login (otherwise the user can't tell whether the URL was bad or auth was bad).\n  - Order matters in vue-router 4 only for ambiguous matches; since `/:pathMatch(.*)*` is the most permissive, it must be LAST.\n\nEDGE CASES:\n  - Typing `/admin` while logged in as STUDENT routes through the guard's role check → `/403`, NOT 404. Confirm by ordering: guard checks `requiresAuth` and `allowedRoles` first for matched routes; only truly unmatched paths fall to the catch-all.\n  - Logout while on `/not-found` (e.g. a stale tab): user stays on the page since `meta.requiresAuth: false`. The 'Back to home' link will resolve to `/login` because `isLoggedIn` is now false."
    },
    {
      "id": "ARCH-010",
      "decision": "Mobile responsiveness pass for <768px: stacked nav, fluid content, horizontal-scroll tables (one consistent strategy), media queries on App.vue and the four heavy views.",
      "rationale": "Viewport meta is already present (verified in index.html). Layouts break below 768px primarily because (a) `.nav-links` is a single flex row with 5–7 buttons that overflows, (b) `.content` has a fixed `max-width: 1200px` but no horizontal padding adjustment, (c) LecturerView's `form-grid` is `1fr 1fr` and squashes inputs, (d) `ResultTable` and `.preview-table` overflow without affordance on some viewports. We pick ONE table strategy — horizontal scroll with a subtle scroll affordance — because it preserves all columns (important for the consolidated scores table which has dynamic exam columns) and a card layout would require duplicating semantic content per row. The brutalist aesthetic favors a stacked nav (no hamburger / no JS toggle) which collapses into a vertical column under the logo at the breakpoint.",
      "affects": [
        "frontend/src/App.vue",
        "frontend/src/components/LecturerView.vue",
        "frontend/src/components/StudentView.vue",
        "frontend/src/components/ResultTable.vue",
        "frontend/src/style.css"
      ],
      "design_notes": "BREAKPOINT: single breakpoint `@media (max-width: 767.98px)`. Use 767.98 (not 768) to avoid double-matching at exactly 768.\n\nAPP.VUE (top nav):\n  - Below breakpoint: `.top-nav` becomes `flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; padding: 0.6rem 1rem; gap: 0.6rem;`.\n  - `.nav-links` becomes `flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: 0.3rem;` and each button becomes full-width with `text-align: left`.\n  - `.user-info` becomes its own line `margin-right: 0; padding-bottom: 0.4rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);`.\n  - `.logo` stays at the top.\n  - `.content` becomes `margin: 1.5rem auto; padding: 0 0.75rem; max-width: 100%;`. (Keep the 1200px cap above the breakpoint.)\n  - DO NOT introduce a hamburger or any JS-driven menu. Stacked is the spec.\n\nSTYLE.CSS (global utilities):\n  - Add a single `.responsive-table-wrap` class (matching `.preview-table-wrap`) with `overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; border: 2px solid var(--color-border);` and a `::after` pseudo or right-side gradient hint to indicate scrollability when content overflows. Keep the affordance subtle (brutalist): a 12px wide right-edge linear-gradient from transparent to `rgba(0,0,0,0.08)` only when scrollable. If implementing the gradient is non-trivial without JS, fall back to a visible `→` chevron hint inside the wrapper for mobile (CSS-only via `@media`).\n  - `.preview-table` (and any other table): below breakpoint, reduce `padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem` and `font-size: 0.8rem` to fit more on small screens before scroll kicks in.\n  - Add a utility `@media (max-width: 767.98px) { .form-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr !important; } }` so any caller using `.form-grid` becomes single-column. (LecturerView's `.full-width` already spans, which becomes a no-op when there is only 1 column — fine.)\n\nLECTURERVIEW.VUE:\n  - `.tab-bar` below breakpoint: change `flex: 1` to allow horizontal scroll. Set `.tab-bar { overflow-x: auto; flex-wrap: nowrap; }` and `.tab-bar button { flex: 0 0 auto; min-width: 110px; }`. Avoid stacking tabs vertically — keeps the brutalist horizontal rhythm.\n  - `.context-selector` becomes `flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch;` below breakpoint.\n  - `.exam-context-status` becomes `flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 0.5rem;` below breakpoint.\n  - The consolidated scores table is already wrapped in `.preview-table-wrap` (horizontal scroll). Verify and keep.\n  - The Results tab's header `<div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between;\">` with the Export button must stack on mobile: extract that inline style into a scoped class `.results-header` and add a media-query rule to switch to `flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch;`.\n\nSTUDENTVIEW.VUE:\n  - Implementor should AUDIT for: fixed widths, inline styles enforcing horizontal layout, table without `.preview-table-wrap`. Apply `.responsive-table-wrap` (or existing `.preview-table-wrap`) to any bare table. Reduce `padding` and stack any `.form-grid` (already handled globally if class is used).\n\nRESULTTABLE.VUE:\n  - Ensure the outer container uses `.preview-table-wrap` (or new `.responsive-table-wrap`) so horizontal scroll kicks in. Do NOT convert rows to cards on mobile — column dimensionality matters here.\n  - If headers wrap awkwardly on mobile, set `white-space: nowrap` on `th` so a column either fits or contributes to overflow (the scroll affordance covers the rest).\n\nNOT IN SCOPE THIS ROUND (P1/P2 — document in `docs/ui_ux_backlog.md` per agent_notes):\n  - Card-per-row table layouts for any view.\n  - Hamburger / slide-in nav.\n  - Tablet breakpoint (768–1023). Acceptable for now to use desktop layout from 768 upward.\n  - Touch-target audit (44px minimum) beyond what naturally falls out of padding bumps.\n\nACCEPTANCE / SPOT-CHECK CRITERIA (validator):\n  - At 360px wide (iPhone SE), `.top-nav` does not horizontally scroll; all buttons are stacked and reachable.\n  - At 360px wide, the consolidated scores table scrolls horizontally without clipping content under the nav.\n  - At 768px exactly, desktop layout applies (single-line nav, two-column form-grid).\n  - No use of `position: fixed` for the nav (keeps brutalist + avoids viewport edge cases on iOS Safari)."
    },
    {
      "id": "ARCH-011",
      "decision": "Per-field inline form validation pattern: a small `useFormValidation()` composable producing reactive `errors`, `touched`, `validateField()`, `validateAll()`, plus a thin `<FieldError>` slot helper. Apply first to LoginView; mark LecturerView question-form, PracticeAuthoring form, AdminView create-user/edit-user, and BulkImportStudents as follow-on consumers.",
      "rationale": "Today, forms either rely on browser-native validation (none on most inputs, no `required` on LoginView/AdminView), or on coarse `notify(...)` toasts thrown from the submit handler. Toasts disappear, give no field-level affordance, and lose accessibility. A single composable keeps validation rules colocated with the field they describe, lets us render the same `aria-invalid` + red-border + inline message pattern everywhere, and avoids reaching for a heavyweight library (vee-validate, vuelidate) for what is a small, schema-free use case. LoginView is the smallest target and the highest-traffic touchpoint, so we land the pattern there first; the other four forms reuse it without redesign.",
      "affects": [
        "frontend/src/composables/useFormValidation.ts",
        "frontend/src/components/FieldError.vue",
        "frontend/src/components/LoginView.vue",
        "frontend/src/style.css"
      ],
      "design_notes": "CREATE `frontend/src/composables/useFormValidation.ts` (new directory). Public API:\n\n```ts\nexport type Validator<T = any> = (value: T) => string | null  // null = ok, string = error message\nexport interface FieldConfig<T = any> { rules: Validator<T>[]; initial?: T }\nexport interface UseFormValidationReturn<F extends Record<string, FieldConfig>> {\n  errors: Record<keyof F, string>            // empty string when no error\n  touched: Record<keyof F, boolean>\n  values: Record<keyof F, any>               // reactive proxy bound by parent via v-model\n  isValid: ComputedRef<boolean>              // true when no errors AND no required-but-empty fields\n  validateField(name: keyof F): boolean      // sets errors[name], returns true if valid\n  validateAll(): boolean                     // validates every field, sets touched=true on all, returns overall valid\n  reset(): void                              // clears touched & errors, restores initial values\n  markTouched(name: keyof F): void           // for @blur binding\n}\nexport function useFormValidation<F extends Record<string, FieldConfig>>(fields: F): UseFormValidationReturn<F>\n```\n\nINTERNAL:\n  - `errors`, `touched`, `values` are `reactive()` objects keyed by field name.\n  - `validateField` runs each validator in `fields[name].rules` against `values[name]`; first non-null return wins (single error message per field). Stores result in `errors[name]` (empty string clears). Returns boolean.\n  - `validateAll` iterates over keys, calls `validateField`, sets all `touched[name] = true`, returns AND of results.\n  - Provide a tiny built-in helpers file as part of the same module:\n    ```ts\n    export const required = (msg = 'Required'): Validator => (v) => (v == null || v === '' ? msg : null)\n    export const minLength = (n: number, msg?: string): Validator<string> => (v) => (v && v.length < n ? (msg || `Min ${n} characters`) : null)\n    export const maxLength = (n: number, msg?: string): Validator<string> => (v) => (v && v.length > n ? (msg || `Max ${n} characters`) : null)\n    ```\n    Do NOT add regex/email validators in this round; LoginView and the lecturer forms don't need them.\n\nCREATE `frontend/src/components/FieldError.vue`:\n  - Props: `message: string` (already-resolved error text), `id?: string` (optional id for `aria-describedby` wiring).\n  - Renders `<p v-if=\"message\" :id=\"id\" class=\"field-error\" role=\"alert\">{{ message }}</p>` — `role=\"alert\"` so screen readers announce it on appearance.\n  - One scoped style: `.field-error { color: var(--color-danger); font-size: 0.8rem; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-weight: 700; }`.\n\nSTYLE.CSS (add ONE new rule, do not touch existing `.input`):\n  - `.input.is-invalid, .input[aria-invalid=\"true\"] { border-color: var(--color-danger); background: var(--color-danger-bg); }`\n  - Reason: invalid state must be visible WITHOUT JavaScript reading classnames; binding `:aria-invalid` doubles as a11y signal and a style hook.\n\nINTEGRATION IN LoginView.vue (the only component touched in this ARCH; other forms are follow-on in a separate ARCH if/when scheduled):\n  - Import: `import { useFormValidation, required } from '../composables/useFormValidation'; import FieldError from './FieldError.vue'`.\n  - Replace the local `username`/`password` refs with:\n    ```ts\n    const form = useFormValidation({\n      username: { rules: [required('Username is required')], initial: '' },\n      password: { rules: [required('Password is required')], initial: '' },\n    })\n    // bind: v-model=\"form.values.username\" / form.values.password\n    ```\n  - In `handleLogin`: prepend `if (!form.validateAll()) return`. Replace the existing top-level `error` ref usage for INVALID-CREDENTIALS scenarios: keep the existing `error` ref for SERVER-side errors (e.g. 401 'Invalid username or password'), but on each input set `aria-invalid=\"!!form.errors.username && form.touched.username\"` and add `@blur=\"form.markTouched('username')\"`. After a failed login response, also call `form.markTouched('username'); form.markTouched('password')` so any subsequent typing surfaces inline messages.\n  - Template: under each input add `<FieldError :message=\"form.touched.username ? form.errors.username : ''\" :id=\"'err-username'\" />` and add `:aria-describedby=\"'err-username'\"` on the input.\n  - The existing server-error `<p v-if=\"error\">` stays (handled by Standardized Error Handling, ARCH-018 — keep inline for the login-specific failure copy in this round).\n  - First-focus: set `autofocus` on the username input (no JS focus call needed; this also addresses P2 item 15 partially as a freebie).\n\nFOLLOW-ON SCOPE (DO NOT IMPLEMENT IN THIS ROUND; recorded so todo-creator can decide whether to defer):\n  - LecturerView's question form (LecturerView.vue ~L513-548): fields `id`, `exam_id` (auto-bound, skip), `target_table` (required when type===DML), `points`, `requirement`, `correct_sql`. Conditional rule (target_table required-if-DML) is supported by passing a closure validator that reads sibling `values`. The composable's `values` proxy is reactive so closures see fresh data.\n  - AdminView create-user form (AdminView.vue ~L123-144): username/password/role — drop the HTML `required` and standardize through the composable.\n  - PracticeAuthoring form (PracticeAuthoring.vue ~L183-205): mirror lecturer pattern.\n  - BulkImportStudents (BulkImportStudents.vue): file presence + extension check (.csv/.xlsx); reuse the same composable.\n  - StudentView SQL submit: OUT OF SCOPE — that's not a form, it's a quiz answer; the existing toast-on-fail pattern is correct for that workflow.\n\nACCESSIBILITY CHECKLIST:\n  - Every input gets `:aria-invalid` bound to a boolean (string 'true'/'false' is what Vue serializes for an attribute; the composable returns boolean which Vue handles correctly).\n  - Every input with a possible error has `:aria-describedby=\"'err-<name>'\"` pointing at the FieldError's id.\n  - Error messages use `role=\"alert\"` so AT users get an immediate announcement.\n  - Focus is NOT auto-moved on submit failure in this round; the form is short. If future forms need it, add a `focusFirstInvalid()` method to the composable.\n\nTESTING (for regression-tester step [7] to author):\n  - Composable unit tests: `required` returns null/string correctly; `validateAll` flips all `touched` true; closure validators see latest `values`; `reset` clears state.\n  - LoginView component test: submit with empty fields -> `aria-invalid=\"true\"` on both inputs, two `<p class=\"field-error\">` rendered, no network call made.\n  - LoginView component test: typing into username clears its error on next `validateField` trigger (i.e. on `@input` debounced or on next submit attempt). Pick ONE trigger: validate on `@blur` after first touch, and on every `@input` AFTER the field has been touched (standard pattern). Do NOT validate on `@input` before first touch — that's noisy.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE:\n  - Async validators (e.g. username availability checks). Not needed.\n  - Cross-field validation beyond closure access (e.g. confirm-password). Not needed.\n  - A schema library (Zod, Yup). Overkill for two-to-five fields per form."
