      "design_notes": "CREATE `frontend/src/components/BackLink.vue` (presentational, no state, no logic beyond click):\n\nProps (TypeScript):\n  - `to: string`              // route path, REQUIRED — explicit destination, not history.back\n  - `label?: string`          // default 'Back'\n  - `ariaLabel?: string`      // default same as label\n\nTemplate:\n```vue\n<template>\n  <router-link :to=\"to\" :aria-label=\"ariaLabel || label || 'Back'\" class=\"back-link\">\n    <span aria-hidden=\"true\">&larr;</span> {{ label || 'Back' }}\n  </router-link>\n</template>\n```\n\nScoped style (brutalist, matches existing `.btn` rhythm without being a button — it's a link semantically):\n```css\n.back-link {\n  display: inline-flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: 0.4rem;\n  font-family: var(--font-mono);\n  font-weight: 700;\n  font-size: 0.85rem;\n  color: var(--color-text);\n  text-decoration: none;\n  padding: 4px 8px;\n  border: 2px solid var(--color-border);\n  background: var(--color-surface);\n  margin-bottom: var(--space-3);\n}\n.back-link:hover { background: var(--color-surface-muted); }\n.back-link:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--color-primary); outline-offset: 2px; }\n```\n\nWhy an explicit `to` instead of `router.back()`:\n  - `router.back()` is fragile: if the user landed deep via a paste or external link, it sends them off-site or to a blank tab state.\n  - Explicit `to` makes the destination predictable and SEO/screen-reader friendly.\n\nINTEGRATION:\n  - `StudentResults.vue`: in the exam-detail mode (`v-if=\"selectedExam\"` block ~L259), there's already a `.back-btn` button calling `goBack`. Replace with `<BackLink to=\"/student/results\" label=\"Back to All Results\" v-if=\"selectedExam\" />` IF goBack just resets `selectedExam = null`, OR keep the goBack logic and convert the button's STYLE to use `.back-link` (since this back-action is INTERNAL state, not a route change). DECISION: keep the existing in-page back logic (it's `selectedExam = null` and a `router.back()` call — verify) but restyle the button using the `.back-link` class. The BackLink COMPONENT is only used where the back action is a true route navigation. Add a one-line scoped `.back-btn { /* compose same look as .back-link */ }` if class reuse isn't trivial.\n  - `PracticeAuthoring.vue`: add `<BackLink to=\"/lecturer\" label=\"Back to Lecturer Dashboard\" />` as the FIRST element inside the root template div, above any existing heading.\n  - `PracticeView.vue`: add `<BackLink to=\"/student\" label=\"Back to Student Dashboard\" />` as the first element inside the root template div.\n  - DO NOT add BackLink to LoginView, AdminView, LecturerView, StudentView, NotFoundView, ForbiddenView (NotFoundView already has its own 'Back to home' affordance from ARCH-009).\n\nROUTE META OPTION (deferred):\n  - Do NOT add `meta.parent` or `meta.breadcrumb` to router.ts in this round. If we later need machine-generated breadcrumbs, we can revisit; for now a hand-placed BackLink with explicit `to` is simpler and unambiguous.\n\nACCESSIBILITY:\n  - The arrow glyph is wrapped in `aria-hidden=\"true\"` so screen readers read the label only.\n  - Default label 'Back' is acceptable but consumers should pass a destination-aware label (the integration list above does).\n\nTESTING (regression-tester):\n  - BackLink renders a router-link with given `to`, default label 'Back', custom label override.\n  - The arrow glyph is present but `aria-hidden`.\n  - Clicking navigates (use vue-router testing harness).\n\nOUT OF SCOPE:\n  - Multi-segment breadcrumb chain.\n  - History-based 'Go back' fallbacks beyond what NotFoundView already does."
