      "id": "ARCH-020",
      "decision": "Codify ADMIN as a wildcard in `router.beforeEach`: when `to.meta.allowedRoles` is defined and does NOT explicitly forbid ADMIN, an ADMIN user is allowed through. Implementation: a single explicit conditional `if (user.role === 'ADMIN' && !to.meta.adminBlocked) return next()` BEFORE the existing role-membership check. Verified current state: the P0 router (ARCH-007) already lists 'ADMIN' explicitly in `allowedRoles` on lecturer-shared routes; this change makes the rule explicit instead of relying on per-route hand-listing.",
      "rationale": "The current router (lines 35, 60) lists ADMIN explicitly alongside LECTURER on `/lecturer` and `/lecturer/practice`. Pattern (b). This works but is brittle: any new route added in the future that forgets to include ADMIN silently breaks the super-set semantics that admins expect. Pattern (a) — ADMIN as wildcard — is the ergonomic default for an internal tool and matches how most systems work (admin can do everything). Allowing an opt-out (`meta.adminBlocked: true`) preserves the ability to gate something away from admins later (e.g. a STUDENT-specific exam-taking screen where ADMIN posing as STUDENT would corrupt analytics). The change is one branch in the guard; the ARCH-007 design_notes get a clarifying paragraph; the per-route allowedRoles arrays can be SIMPLIFIED in a follow-up (e.g. /lecturer becomes `allowedRoles: ['LECTURER']` because the wildcard covers ADMIN) but we leave them as-is in this round for backwards compatibility and only add the wildcard rule.",
      "affects": [
        "frontend/src/router.ts"
      ],
      "design_notes": "VERIFIED STATE OF router.ts AS OF 2026-05-22 (from a fresh read):\n  - `/login` -> `meta: { requiresAuth: false }`\n  - `/student` -> `{ requiresAuth: true, allowedRoles: ['STUDENT'] }`\n  - `/lecturer` -> `{ requiresAuth: true, allowedRoles: ['ADMIN', 'LECTURER'] }`   <-- ADMIN listed explicitly\n  - `/admin` -> `{ requiresAuth: true, allowedRoles: ['ADMIN'] }`\n  - `/practice` -> `{ requiresAuth: true, allowedRoles: ['STUDENT'] }`\n  - `/student/results` -> `{ requiresAuth: true, allowedRoles: ['STUDENT'] }`\n  - `/lecturer/practice` -> `{ requiresAuth: true, allowedRoles: ['ADMIN', 'LECTURER'] }`   <-- ADMIN listed explicitly\n  - `/dashboard` -> redirect by role (no meta needed beyond default)\n  - `/403`, `/:pathMatch(.*)*` -> `{ requiresAuth: false }`\n  - beforeEach guard already has 4 working rules including the named-route exception from TODO-033.\n\nSO THE BUG IS: there is no actual bug TODAY (ADMIN can reach /lecturer because they're listed). The brief asks us to codify the rule so we don't regress when future routes are added and someone forgets to list ADMIN. The fix is forward-looking, not corrective.\n\nCHANGE TO router.ts:\n\n  1. Extend `RouteMeta` augmentation:\n     ```ts\n     declare module 'vue-router' {\n       interface RouteMeta {\n         requiresAuth: boolean\n         allowedRoles?: Array<'ADMIN' | 'LECTURER' | 'STUDENT'>\n         /** Block ADMIN's wildcard access. Defaults to false. Use only for role-purist screens (e.g. STUDENT exam-taking UI). */\n         adminBlocked?: boolean\n       }\n     }\n     ```\n\n  2. Modify the Rule 3 block in the existing `beforeEach` so ADMIN is allowed through unless `adminBlocked` is set:\n     ```ts\n     // Rule 3: role check\n     if (to.meta.allowedRoles && user.value) {\n       const role = user.value.role\n       // ADMIN wildcard: allowed everywhere unless the route explicitly opts out.\n       if (role === 'ADMIN' && !to.meta.adminBlocked) {\n         next()\n         return\n       }\n       if (!to.meta.allowedRoles.includes(role)) {\n         next({ name: 'forbidden' })\n         return\n       }\n     }\n     ```\n\n  3. DOC COMMENT block above the beforeEach, summarizing the four rules and the wildcard. Concise — 6-10 lines.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES (and does NOT) change:\n  - Today: ADMIN typing /student is forbidden (role not in ['STUDENT']) and goes to /403.\n  - After: ADMIN typing /student is ALLOWED (wildcard) and sees the StudentView.\n  - This is the desired ergonomic for an internal-tool admin who occasionally needs to verify student-facing UI. To preserve the old behavior on STUDENT-only screens, set `meta.adminBlocked: true` on `/student`, `/practice`, `/student/results`. We do this for `/practice` ONLY (where ADMIN viewing causes scoring writes against the admin's user_id, polluting analytics) — see below.\n  - LECTURER typing /student still forbidden — wildcard only applies to ADMIN.\n  - LECTURER typing /admin still forbidden — wildcard is admin-on-everything-else, not the inverse.\n\nPER-ROUTE adminBlocked DECISIONS (audit):\n  - `/student` -> `adminBlocked: false` (allow admin to inspect). Acceptable because viewing the student dashboard does not write data.\n  - `/practice` -> `adminBlocked: TRUE`. Reason: PracticeView posts answers via `/api/v1/practice/submit` which records the submitting user_id; an admin click would create spurious 'attempts' attributed to the admin user. Block to prevent this.\n  - `/student/results` -> `adminBlocked: false` (read-only, useful for support).\n  - `/lecturer`, `/lecturer/practice` -> no change (admin already in allowedRoles; wildcard or not, they're in).\n  - `/admin` -> only allowedRoles=['ADMIN'], wildcard is moot.\n\nALSO: simplify the per-route `allowedRoles` lists to remove the redundant 'ADMIN' entry on /lecturer and /lecturer/practice (no behavior change because of the wildcard, but reduces cognitive load):\n  - `/lecturer`: `allowedRoles: ['LECTURER']`\n  - `/lecturer/practice`: `allowedRoles: ['LECTURER']`\n  Verifier: ADMIN still reaches them via the wildcard. If the validator is concerned about the change, leaving the explicit ADMIN entries is harmless (the wildcard short-circuits before the includes check). Recommend simplifying for clarity. Implementor judgment.\n\nUPDATE arch_decisions ARCH-007 design_notes (text-only):\n  - Add a paragraph at the end of design_notes pointing to ARCH-020 and saying 'ADMIN is now an implicit wildcard via the Rule 3 short-circuit; per-route `adminBlocked: true` opts a route out for analytics or correctness reasons.' (Implementor: append, do not rewrite existing content.)\n\nTESTING (regression-tester additions to router.test.ts):\n  - Logged-in ADMIN -> /lecturer -> allowed (already passes; ensure still passes).\n  - Logged-in ADMIN -> /student -> allowed (NEW behavior; previously was forbidden).\n  - Logged-in ADMIN -> /practice -> redirected to /403 because meta.adminBlocked=true (NEW behavior).\n  - Logged-in LECTURER -> /student -> still redirected to /403 (unchanged).\n  - Logged-in STUDENT -> /admin -> still redirected to /403 (unchanged).\n  - Logged-in ADMIN -> /admin -> allowed (unchanged).\n\nNON-GOALS:\n  - No new RBAC layer.\n  - No backend authorization changes. ADMIN can already hit every backend endpoint per the existing JWT-claim authorization in Go controllers; this change just brings the frontend in line.\n  - No 'super-admin' tier beyond ADMIN."
